# scripts/verify.conf — this project's values for the verify tiers.
#
# Copy this file to scripts/verify.conf and fill in what applies. Every tier
# sources it (it is bash), so a framework upgrade never needs an edit to
# verify-full.sh or verify-fast.sh. Without the file, the tiers behave exactly as
# before, plus timing, a run record and informational lines.
#
# Having the file at all switches ON one check: the working tree must not change
# while verify-full.sh runs (a step that edits a tracked file, or leaves a new
# file that is not git-ignored, turns the gate RED). So git-ignore every artifact
# your tests write before you copy this file. Run records go to .verify/, which
# the recorder always leaves out of the tree hash.
#
# LOADING. Both tiers check this file with `bash -n` first. A syntax error stops
# them before any step runs: verify-full.sh is RED, verify-fast.sh exits 2. Bash
# keeps whatever it read before the error, so carrying on would silently drop the
# rest of your settings. A last line that returns non-zero, such as
# `[ -n "$X" ] && VAR=…`, is not an error. LIMIT: a conf that reads an unset
# variable (`$CI_NAME` with no default) ends the tier with bash's own message.
#
# QUOTING. Commands that must see $VERIFY_JUNIT_DIR are run with `eval`, once per
# step, so write them in SINGLE quotes: the variable then expands when the step
# runs, inside that step's fresh directory. In double quotes it would expand now,
# while it is still unset.

# ---- the steps ----------------------------------------------------------------
# STEPS replaces the list in verify-full.sh. Keep the framework's own steps
# (hooks, ci-examples, scar-forge, coordinator, framework) unless you know why not.
# An empty STEPS is RED ("no steps configured"), and two steps listed in
# VERIFY_TEST_STEPS may not share a label.
#
# STEPS=(
#   'lint|<your lint command>'
#   'format|<your format --check command>'
#   'tests|<your full test command, writing JUnit XML into $VERIFY_JUNIT_DIR>'
#   'hooks|python3 -m pytest tests/hooks/ -q'
#   'ci-examples|python3 -m pytest tests/ci/ -q'
#   'scar-forge|python3 -m pytest tests/scars/ -q && python3 scripts/scar_status.py'
#   'coordinator|python3 -m pytest tests/coordinator/ -q'
#   'framework|python3 -m pytest tests/framework/ -q && python3 scripts/check_neutrality.py && python3 scripts/memory_lint.py'
# )
#
# LINT and TEST replace the fast tier's commands. They are arrays, expanded when
# this file is sourced (VERIFY_JUNIT_DIR is already set by then), and the paths
# you pass to verify-fast.sh are appended to both.
#
# LINT=(<your lint command>)
# TEST=(<your test command> --junitxml="$VERIFY_JUNIT_DIR/fast.xml")

# ---- counts: executed == collected ----------------------------------------------
# Labels of the steps that run tests, space-separated. Each one must write JUnit
# XML into $VERIFY_JUNIT_DIR. A listed step that writes no report, or a report
# with 0 test cases, is RED. Empty means no JUnit, zero-case or count checks.
#
# VERIFY_TEST_STEPS="tests"
#
# Prints the number of tests the full run should report (the last line's first
# integer is read). If the reported total differs, the gate is RED with
# "reported N of M collected". Unset: the gate says
# "counts: not configured — executed == collected NOT checked".
# For a pytest stack, for example:
#
# VERIFY_COLLECT_CMD='python3 -m pytest --collect-only -q | tail -1'

# ---- the handoff tier (scripts/verify-handoff.sh) — opt-in -------------------------
# With VERIFY_HANDOFF_TIER=on (exactly that word), scripts/verify-handoff.sh runs
# the tests a machine selects from the diff instead of the whole suite, and says
# "ran N of M". Anything else, or no conf: it prints HANDOFF TIER NOT CONFIGURED,
# exits 3, and scripts/verify-full.sh before every handoff stays in force.
# `scripts/verify-handoff.sh --status` prints "opted in" or "not opted in".
# Opted in, the tier also needs VERIFY_SELECT_CMD, VERIFY_FULL_TEST_CMD and
# VERIFY_COLLECT_CMD (above), or it is NOT CONFIGURED; and it is never GREEN.
#
# VERIFY_HANDOFF_TIER=on
#
# The diff is taken against the merge base of this ref and HEAD, plus staged,
# unstaged and untracked (not ignored) files. A ref that does not resolve, or
# that shares no history with HEAD, runs the full set and says so. Default:
#
# VERIFY_BASE_REF=origin/main
#
# Your selector. It gets the selected paths as separate arguments after the
# command, runs the tests related to them, and writes JUnit XML into
# $VERIFY_JUNIT_DIR (single quotes, as for STEPS). The paths are the diff plus any
# you name, relative to the project root; they can include DELETED files, so the
# selector must accept a path that no longer exists. A path starting with `-`
# arrives as `./-…`. With an empty selection the selector is never called (the
# full set runs instead), because a test runner given no paths runs everything.
# The selector's own correctness is yours; the tier guarantees its input is
# never narrower than the diff, that 0 tests is a failure, and that the label is
# honest. Its pytest arguments are checked against the bans below. For example,
# pytest's own collection filtered by the changed paths:
#
# VERIFY_SELECT_CMD='python3 scripts/pick_tests.py'
#
# The full collected set, writing JUnit XML; run when the selection cannot be
# trusted (see below). It must report as many tests as VERIFY_COLLECT_CMD counts.
#
# VERIFY_FULL_TEST_CMD='python3 -m pytest -q --junitxml="$VERIFY_JUNIT_DIR/full.xml"'
#
# Space-separated globs for inputs your selector cannot trace to tests (data
# files, config, docs that tests read). A change to any of them runs the full
# set. EMPTY MEANS THE FULL SET EVERY TIME: until you declare these, the tier
# never selects. `*` crosses `/`, `**/x` also matches a top-level x, and a plain
# directory (`doc` or `doc/`) covers everything under it.
#
# VERIFY_UNTRACKED_INPUTS='*.md *.toml *.json config/**'
#
# Do not name your selector after a Python module (scripts/select.py would shadow
# the stdlib `select` for your own script); the recorder itself runs isolated.

# ---- bans and the declared filter allow-list --------------------------------------
# The built-in bans are pytest's. They are refused (exit 2, before any step runs)
# in the arguments of pytest in every configured command and in
# VERIFY_COLLECT_CMD, in every argument passed to verify-fast.sh, and in
# PYTEST_ADDOPTS (set in a command or in the environment), because each can
# report success while executing nothing:
#   --lf* --last-failed* --ff* --failed-first* --sw* --stepwise*   (so --sw-skip,
#   --sw-reset, --lfnf too)   -x --exitfirst --maxfail[=n] --bail[=n]
#   --reruns* --retries --retry --flake-finder --cache_test_results* --cache-test-results*
#   -k / -m / --deselect / --ignore / --ignore-glob   (filters)
# Clustered and attached forms count too: -xq, -qk smoke, -mslow, -m"not slow".
# --cache-clear is allowed.
#
# pytest is found where a command runs it: `pytest`, `py.test`, `.venv/bin/pytest`,
# and behind `env`, `uv run`, `coverage run` and `python3 -m pytest` / `-mpytest`.
# A word that is only an argument (`make -C tools/pytest`, `echo pytest -x`) is not.
# Other programs are never scanned: `git commit -m`, `tar -x`, `go test -timeout`.
#
# Any OTHER test runner is not flag-scanned: the gate does not guess its flags.
# List the ones you use here, and each step that runs one prints
#   limit: step <label> runs <runner>; its flags are not scanned — declare bans in VERIFY_EXTRA_BANNED
# A step listed in VERIFY_TEST_STEPS that runs no pytest prints the same line with
# "no known runner". The line is also kept in the record's warnings; the verdict
# does not change. The pytest family is always scanned, listed or not.
#
# VERIFY_TEST_RUNNERS=(pytest py.test)
# VERIFY_TEST_RUNNERS=(pytest py.test jest "go test" "cargo nextest" "bazel test")
#
# LIMIT: flags inside `eval` or a shell's `-c` string, flags held in a variable
# (`pytest $FLAGS`), and pytest behind any other wrapper (`timeout 600 pytest`,
# `poetry run pytest`, `npx`, `tox`) are not seen. eval and -c print a `limit:`
# line; another wrapper prints one only in a step listed in VERIFY_TEST_STEPS.
# Unknown interpreters (`pypy3 -m pytest`, `py -m pytest`) and a renamed copy or
# alias of pytest (`pytest3`) are treated the same way as another wrapper.
# Arguments typed at verify-fast.sh are always checked against pytest's table,
# whatever your runner is, so your runner's own flags pass there silently.
# `--flake-runs` passes silently; it repeats nothing without `--flake-finder`,
# which is banned. `--force-flaky` and `--max-runs` are always banned: a retried
# test is still one test, so the count check cannot see a retry.
#
# Your runner's own bans (space-separated), refused in every program, in every
# step. You can only add, never remove. A `--flag` also matches `--flag=value`.
#
# VERIFY_EXTRA_BANNED=''
#
# Examples (check them against your tool's docs; a short flag such as jest's -b
# would be refused in every program too, so prefer the long form):
# jest:           VERIFY_EXTRA_BANNED='--onlyFailures --bail --onlyChanged'
# go:             VERIFY_EXTRA_BANNED='-failfast -run'
# cargo nextest:  VERIFY_EXTRA_BANNED='--retries'
# bazel:          VERIFY_EXTRA_BANNED='--cache_test_results --flaky_test_attempts'
#
# A marker or deselect filter that is genuinely part of how THIS project runs its
# suite may be declared here, with the reason, as "<filter exactly as written in
# the step command>|<reason>". Only -m, -k, --deselect, --ignore and --ignore-glob
# can be declared, and the value must match exactly. A declared filter is printed
# on every run and kept in the record. A declared filter must also be applied by
# VERIFY_COLLECT_CMD, or the count check reports a mismatch. Never allowed, even
# with a reason: --lf, --last-failed, --ff, -x, --exitfirst, --maxfail, --bail,
# --sw/--stepwise, retries, cached results.
# The allow-list covers your configured step commands only, never arguments typed
# at verify-fast.sh.
#
# VERIFY_ALLOWED_FILTERS=(
#   '-m "not real_sleep"|real-sleep tests run in the nightly job'
# )

# ---- time budgets: warnings only, never a verdict ----------------------------------
# A step slower than this many seconds gets a WARN line.
#
# VERIFY_STEP_BUDGET_S=900
#
# Per-test budgets by size class, read from each JUnit case's time. A test over its
# class budget gets a WARN line naming the step and the test; the ten slowest are
# listed under "slowest tests:".
#
# VERIFY_TEST_BUDGET_SMALL_S=1
# VERIFY_TEST_BUDGET_MEDIUM_S=10
# VERIFY_TEST_BUDGET_LARGE_S=60
#
# Size rules: space-separated glob=class pairs, matched in order against each
# test's file part (the JUnit `file` attribute, or else the classname with dots
# turned into slashes). The first match wins; a test that matches nothing is small.
#
# VERIFY_TEST_SIZE_RULES='tests/integration/*=medium tests/e2e/*=large'

# ---- where the run happened ---------------------------------------------------------
# Set by a CI runner in its environment (not here). Empty: the record says
# local:<first 12 hex of sha256(hostname)>; the hostname itself, which often
# carries a person's name, is never recorded.
#
# VERIFY_RUNNER_ID
